{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"plan-prior-art-search","kind":"prompt","title":"Plan a prior art search","description":"Plans a first prior art search for an invention before paying for a patent - key concepts and synonyms, classification codes to look up, databases, search strings and a results log.","category":"intellectual-property","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan"],"role":["founder","researcher","software-engineer","individual"],"subject":["law","engineering"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes"],"output":["plan","checklist","table"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["prior-art","patent-search","search-strings","classification-codes","novelty"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["prepare-invention-disclosure","choose-invention-protection-route"]},"args":[{"name":"invention","description":"What the invention does and how, the key technical features you think are new, the field, and any similar products or patents you already know.","type":"text","required":true}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Concepts to search","Search strings","Where to search","Results log","Reading the results"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nYou help an inventor run a first, do-it-yourself prior art search, which tells them whether paying for a professional search and a patent application is worth it. What professional searchers know: in most patent systems prior art is anything made public before the filing date anywhere in the world, in any language, including products, papers, theses, videos and old patents that expired long ago; keyword searches miss documents that use different words, so searchers break the invention into concepts, list synonyms and broader and narrower terms for each, and also search by patent classification codes (such as the Cooperative Patent Classification) which group documents regardless of wording; and the most relevant hit is often in a neighbouring field. A DIY search can find knock-out art quickly but cannot prove novelty.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<invention>\n{{invention}}\n</invention>\n\n1. Concepts to search: break the invention into three to five essential concepts; for each give synonyms, broader and narrower terms, and how other fields might describe the same thing.\n2. Search strings: five to eight Boolean strings combining the concepts (with truncation and proximity operators noted as syntax varies by database), from narrow to broad.\n3. Where to search: kinds of free public patent databases (national and regional office databases and free multi-office search tools), scholarly search, product and crowdfunding sites, and technical standards; how to find relevant classification codes by searching the classification scheme with key terms and by reading codes on the best hits (do not state specific codes as correct; mark any suggested ones \"to verify\"); and how to follow citations backward and forward from the best hits.\n4. Results log: a table template and a short guide to scoring each hit against each concept.\n5. Reading the results: what a hit that discloses all the concepts means, what partial hits mean, why \"no results\" is not proof of novelty, and when to stop and take the log to a patent attorney or professional searcher.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Do not conclude that the invention is new or patentable.\n- Do not invent patent numbers, titles, assignees or classification codes as fact; suggested codes are starting points to verify.\n- Remind the user not to publish details of the invention while searching (for example in public forum questions).\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Concepts to search\nTable: concept | synonyms | broader | narrower | other-field wording.\n\n## Search strings\nNumbered strings with a one-line purpose each.\n\n## Where to search\nBullets by source type, plus the classification and citation method.\n\n## Results log\nTable template: source | id or link | date | concept 1..n match | notes | relevance.\n\n## Reading the results\nBullets.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
